{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the glory of YHWH dwelt upon Mount Sinai.</b> This is what He mentioned above (Exodus 20:1): ״וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה״ — and there I wrote and expanded the explanation. And what it says, ״ויכסהו הענן ששת ימים״ — 'and the cloud covered it six days' — this is from the first of the month of Sivan until the sixth of it, and then the Torah was given." "<b>And He called to Moses on the seventh day.</b> This was on the morrow after the giving of the Torah, and he remained there forty days and forty nights, which is what is said: ״והיה משה בהר ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה״. And the six days that he was covered by the cloud were included in the forty. And if you should ask: since Moses remained there and did not descend until the seventeenth of Tammuz, which completes the forty, why was this call necessary — but we find among our Rabbis, of blessed memory, several opinions on this. As they said in the first chapter of Yoma: ״ויקרא אל משה ביום השביעי״ — 'And He called to Moses on the seventh day' — the verse came only to bestow honor upon Moses — the words of Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Natan says: the verse came only to purge the food and drink in his bowels, so as to make him like the ministering angels. Rabbi Matya ben Harash says: the verse came only to instill fear in him, so that the Torah would be given in awe and dread, in trembling and quaking, as it is said (Psalms 2:11): ״עבדו את ה' ביראה וגילו ברעדה״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> Come and see that all the work of Creation was created in six days, while the Torah was given after forty days. Happy is he who gives up the labor of six and takes up the labor of forty. And it appears to me regarding this number, for it is well known that Moses attained the ultimate attainment in the wisdom of the Torah that the human species can attain, but it is impossible for the human species to reach the culmination of that attainment in less than this amount of time; therefore he needed to remain there to learn the Torah for forty days and forty nights. And from this our Rabbis, of blessed memory, were accustomed to say: ״תנא מניה ארבעין זמנין״ — 'he counted from it forty times' — ״ודמיא ליה כמאן דמנחא בכיסתיה״ — 'and it is like one who has it resting in his pouch.'" "<b>Also,</b> it appears to me that by the merit of those forty days he merited the radiance of the skin of his face for the forty years that he lived thereafter, for all his years were one hundred and twenty years, and that great splendor did not depart from him until the day of his death, as Scripture testified concerning him (Deuteronomy 34:7): ״לא כהתה עינו ולא נס לחה״. And the days he stood on the mountain — three times forty days — were one hundred and twenty days, corresponding to the number of the years during which the forty first days were given on the first tablets to learn the explanation of the Torah, the forty second days to pray concerning the matter of the calf, and the forty third days to receive the second tablets." "<b>From within the cloud.</b> Because Moses was covered by the cloud for all six days but did not enter inside it, therefore Scripture says that now, on the seventh day, He called to Moses from within the cloud and brought him inside it, in the manner of what it says: ״ויבא משה בתוך הענן״. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, already expounded in the first chapter of Yoma: He made a path within it. It says here ״בתוך הענן״ — 'in the midst of the cloud' — and it says of the sea (Exodus 14:16): ״ויבאו בני ישראל בתוך הים״ — 'and the children of Israel came into the midst of the sea.' Just as there it was a path, as it is said (ibid.): ״והמים להם חומה״ and so on — so too here, a path."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:50.547494840Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}